  "executive_summary": "This paper investigates the geometrically maximal abelian coverings of the modular curve $X_0(p)$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$, specifically focusing on the open subscheme $Y_0(p)$ obtained by removing the cusps. While Mazur previously established that the Shimura covering $X_2(p) \\to X_0(p)$ is the geometrically maximal unramified abelian covering of the full curve $X_0(p)$, the authors address the distinct case where ramification is allowed at the cusps. They prove that there exists a cyclic covering $X'_2(p) \\to X_0(p)$ of degree $2N_p$, where $N_p = (p-1)/\\gcd(p-1, 12)$, which is geometrically maximal for $Y_0(p)$. This new covering $X'_2(p)$ factors through the Shimura curve $X_2(p)$ via a degree-two covering $\\pi_p$ that is unramified outside the cusps. The construction relies on the theory of generalized Dedekind eta functions (Siegel functions) to explicitly define the function field of $X'_2(p)$ as a quadratic extension of $\\mathbb{Q}(X_2(p))$. The authors demonstrate that the square of a specific modular function $F_h$, constructed from products of eta functions, generates this extension. They verify that this function has odd order at the cusps and is constant elsewhere, ensuring the required ramification profile. The result extends the understanding of the abelian fundamental group of modular curves by incorporating the generalized Jacobian with modulus at the cusps. The paper provides a complete classification of this maximal extension for all primes $p$, handling the special case $p \\equiv 11 \\pmod{12}$ separately due to the structure of the relevant congruence subgroups. This work bridges the gap between the unramified theory of Mazur and the more general theory of abelian coverings with controlled ramification, offering explicit tools for studying the arithmetic of these curves.",
